<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1186">1186</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh de Lacy was an Anglo-Norman baron who in 1172 received from Henry II the former Kingdom of Mide as the Lordship of Meath, to balance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>'s power in Leinster. He built motte castles and Trim Castle, settled English tenants and served as the king's governor in Ireland. His marriage to a daughter of the High King Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair aroused royal suspicion. He was killed at Durrow in 1186.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath.jpg" alt="Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerald of Wales · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hugh_de_Lacy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hugh de Lacy was born before 1135, the son of Gilbert de Lacy of Ewyas Lacy, Weobley and Ludlow, and held large estates in Herefordshire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>. He is said to have disputed lands in Herefordshire with Josce de Dinan in 1154; he held his father's lands before 1163, and in 1165–66 he held fifty-eight and three-quarters knight's fees. His first wife, Rohese of Monmouth, was a granddaughter of Gilbert Fitz Richard, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> was, and their children included Walter de Lacy and Hugh de Lacy, 1st Earl of Ulster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>De Lacy crossed to Ireland with Henry II in October 1171 and early in 1172 was sent to receive the submission of Rory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a>. Before Henry left he received Meath for the service of fifty knights, with almost royal authority, and charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, making him in effect the first Viceroy of Ireland. Tighearnán <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-ruairc">Ó Ruairc</a>, King of Bréifne, who ruled Meath, would not concede it. At a parley on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-ward">Hill of Ward</a> an interpreter was killed by a blow aimed at Lacy, and Ó Ruairc was speared and beheaded; the Annals of the Four Masters say he was treacherously slain.</p><p>Lacy began securing Meath with castles such as Trim, put in the charge of Hugh Tyrrel, then the following year fought for Henry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, holding Verneuil against Louis VII for a month before it capitulated. Sent back as procurator-general in 1177, after the death of Richard de Clare, he had his grant confirmed with Offaly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> and Wicklow added. He built many castles while leaving the Irish in possession of their lands, and was accused of intending to seize the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Recalled in 1181 for marrying Róis Ní Chonchobair without the king's permission, he returned the next winter with a royal clerk, Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, as coadjutor. In 1185 Prince John complained that Lacy would not let the Irish pay tribute. He was killed by Gilla-Gan-Mathiar O'Maidhaigh while supervising a motte castle at Durrow, and John was sent to take possession of his lands. Buried first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrow-abbey">Durrow Abbey</a>, his remains were disputed between Bective Abbey and St Thomas's Abbey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, until in 1205 they were laid in his first wife's tomb at St Thomas's.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its lands passed into the Lordship of Meath granted to <b>Hugh de Lacy</b> in 1172, and its name survives in Irish place-names.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brega">Kingdom of Brega</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1172 Henry II granted it to <b>Hugh de Lacy</b>, creating the Lordship of Meath, and the native line survived only as minor lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Kingdom of Mide</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Norman invasion much of southern Tethba was granted to <b>Hugh de Lacy</b> within the Lordship of Meath.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tethba">Kingdom of Tethba</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1172 Henry II granted the former Kingdom of Mide to <b>Hugh de Lacy</b> to balance Strongbow's power in Leinster.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-meath">Lordship of Meath</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Herefordshire, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Leinster, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.08,-2.75&amp;year=1176" aria-label="Herefordshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hugh de Lacy, Lord of Meath"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4856 5672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4856 5672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.08,-2.75&amp;year=1176">What was here in 1176? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
